 Notes on bootstrapping a new crosscompiler environment.

 You can easily build a standalone version of collect-aros from the configured AROS
source tree using 'make standalone-collect-aros' command.

 If you are bootstrapping a build for a new architecture, and don't have a compiler for
the target at all, you'll be unable to configure the tree. In this case you need to do the
following:

1. Copy env.h.in file as env.h and replace @aros_target_cpu@ with your CPU and @aros_object_format@
   with correct object format name.
2. Run "make USER_CFLAGS=-D_STANDALONE_" right in this directory. If you're building on MinGW32,
   this command won't work. Use "make CC=gcc AROS_HOST_ARCH=mingw32".

 You need to perform the following steps to create a new crosscompiler from scratch:

1. Download patches from contrib/gnu/gcc and contrib/gnu/binutils
2. Download corresponding binutils and gcc source archives. Note that for i386-aros target
   you may use the latest binutils because support for this target is already in mainstream.
3. Download AROS SDK from AROS web site.
4. Patch, configure, build and install binutils with --target=<cpu>-aros
5. Extract include and lib directories from SDK into /usr/local/<cpu>-aros/ directory.
   This assumes that you didn't specify any --prefix while configuring your tools.
   Otherwise specify yor prefix instead of /usr/local. Rename 'include' to 'sys-include'.
   If you're on Windows, check also lib/libm.a and lib/libz.a. Originally they are symlinks
   to libarosm.a and libarosz.a respectively, and they are extracted as zero-length files,
   which cause linker to fail. Replace them with plain copies of libarosm.a and libarosz.a.
6. Build collect-aros and install into <prefix>/<cpu>-aros/bin directory
   (/usr/local/i386-aros/bin for example). In theory you can put it into any directory in
   your path (like /usr/local/bin), but this will prevent multiple crosstoolchains from
   coexisting (i386-aros and ppc-aros for example).
7. Patch, configure, build and install gcc. That's all.

 Debug stripping regression (AArch64 LLVM cross tools):

 python3 tools/collect-aros/tests/debug-strip.py \
   --collect-aros /path/to/configured/collect-aros \
   --clang /path/to/cross/clang --output /path/to/new/test-directory

 The test links an ELF object containing embedded bitcode and debug information.
 Both --strip-debug and -S must remove final debug sections while retaining
 allocated data, symbols and relocations. An unstripped control retains debug
 information; an incremental link also exercises debug stripping. This is a
 host cross-link test, not target runtime qualification.

 Incremental link dependencies:

 When the raw linker supports --dependency-file, the build rules set
 COLLECT_AROS_DEPENDENCIES to the output's .ld.d sidecar. collect-aros merges
 the real inputs from both link stages, including compiler-driver defaults,
 resolved archives and extra objects selected during symbol processing. It
 excludes its generated script and intermediate object. A successful link
 atomically publishes the sidecar; a failed link removes its incomplete output
 and leaves the previous sidecar intact. COLLECT_AROS_DEPENDENCY_TARGET can
 name the installed destination when the build links to a temporary output.

 Program, module and kernel-object rules include these dependencies. Existing
 outputs without sidecars relink once. Updating a compiler-default library
 therefore relinks its consumers, while an unrelated library does not. Raw
 linkers without dependency-file support retain the existing explicit-library
 prerequisites; that fallback does not cover implicit compiler libraries.

 python3 tools/collect-aros/tests/link-dependencies.py \
   --env-header /path/to/configured/collect-aros/env.h \
   --clang /path/to/cross/clang --make /path/to/gnu/make \
   --output /path/to/new/test-directory

 The test builds the production collector and expands the production make
 rules. Real AArch64 ELF data proves that the previous explicit-only rules
 leave consumers stale, and that implicit, archlibs and ldflags archive changes
 update all three output forms. It also checks no-change and unrelated-archive
 stability, initial sidecar migration, final-stage inputs, failed publication,
 partial links and make escaping. This is host cross-link evidence; it does
 not qualify a boot image or the runtime behavior of the linked code.
